. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * Please add a verbal description of your model here
. 
. * We investigate whether having a daughter is associated with more traditiona
> l gender attitudes. The dependent variable is a composite index created from:
. * 1. "A child under 3 suffers if the mother works" (plh0302_v1, plh0302_v2)
. * 2. "Best if man and woman work the same amount to share responsibility" (pl
> h0308_v1, plh0308_v2)
. 
. *The variables measuring this are available in the years 2012 and 2018. Based
>  on the specification listed in our PAP, we had initially proposed to run the
>  analysis seperately for these years as they also individually include furthe
> r measures of gender attitudes. However, for the sake of choosing our preferr
> ed estimation, we opt here to the one that pools both years as to increase th
> e number of observations, which reflects analysis 2 from the PAP, i.e.: 2012 
> & 2018 combined: Individuals who had at least one child by the year of intere
> st and responded to the 2 questions available both in 2012 and 2018.
. 
. * Due to different coding formats across years, we harmonize and rescale the 
> items so that higher values consistently indicate more traditional gender att
> itudes. 
. * In the pre-analysis plan, we stated we would z-score standardize gender att
> itude variables. However, upon closer inspection of the survey item formats a
> cross years, we found that harmonizing the original scales into a common 1–4 
> interpretation (higher = more traditional) yielded a more intuitive and trans
> parent metric. This transformation preserves comparability across years and f
> acilitates interpretation of coefficients in the analysis. We maintain consis
> tency in directionality and range across waves, ensuring validity of cross-te
> mporal comparisons.
. 
. * Interpretation:
. * - Coefficient on d_daughter captures whether having at least one daughter i
> s associated with more traditional gender role attitudes.
. * - Controls include sex, age, education, migration background, region (East)
> , marital status, number of children, number of children under 16, labor forc
> e status, monthly income, working hours, and survey year fixed effects.
. * - Robust standard errors are used.
. 
. * Disclaimer:
. * In our PAP, we proposed three alternative model specifications (1)w/o contr
> ols, 2)with core controls and 3)with additional controls) to test the hypothe
> sis. However, as per the current request to report only a single regression a
> nd associated p-value, we have selected the specification we consider most ap
> propriate and transparent for capturing the effect. In our PAP this specifica
> tion is listed as sepcification 2, i.e. the model with core controls. As expl
> ained on page 2, sentence one in our PAP, it includes key demographic charact
> eristics of the respondent, including age, gender, education, migration backg
> round, East/West Germany indicator, employment variables (where this is not t
> he outcome), and marital status. Note that we forgot to list the marital stat
> us and the education variable in our table but included it here now, as we di
> d mention it in our text. This model is reported and submitted in the present
>  do-file. The results from the alternative specifications mentioned in the PA
> P are available upon request. 
. 
